  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>State media coverage of the UNGA week framed Iran's participation around a "comprehensive peace initiative" and the 10-point roadmap. Araghchi addressed the General Assembly from a stated position of deterrence credibility following the September 2 retaliatory strikes. IRIB broadcast President Pezeshkian's UNGA address at length, presenting his New York appearance as an assertion of international legitimacy rather than a response to economic pressure. Blockade economic consequences — fuel shortages, inflationary spiral, port congestion — appeared in domestic coverage but were consistently attributed to US "economic warfare" rather than Iranian policy choices. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>IRGC-aligned outlets — Javan and the more hawkish editorial line at Tasnim — characterized the UNGA engagement with skepticism, with op-eds describing the US diplomatic approach as a potential delay operation. IRGC commentary elevated the September 2 strike as the correct deterrence precedent for responding to future US provocations, not as a step toward negotiation. The Hormuz closure appeared in these outlets as a "defensive response" to what they described as an illegal American naval siege, not an Iranian escalatory choice. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Araghchi's September 29 meeting with Qatari mediators in Doha received substantially less domestic coverage than the September 23 UNGA appearance — a pattern consistent with Iranian state media treating bilateral back-channel contacts as more sensitive than multilateral forum appearances. The divergence between official diplomatic engagement and IRGC-media skepticism is an ongoing internal tension in Tehran's public communications about the talks track. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

